        {
          // IMPLEMENTED: readable notes — loss adjustment report
          "id": "simon_hartley_report_envelope",
          "type": "notes",
          "name": "Loss Adjustment Report (Fairbridge Associates)",
          "takeable": false,
          "readable": true,
          "puzzle_graph_role": "clue",
          "puzzle_graph_aim": "make_recommendation",
          "puzzle_graph_reveals": "£8.2M quantum breakdown with the contested business interruption, module replacement and betterment items; Insurance Act 2015 sections 10 and 11 and the negotiated A2 settlement (about £6.1M): informs coverage position selection",
          "text": "FAIRBRIDGE ASSOCIATES: Loss Adjustment Report, MC-2023-ALBE-007\nPrepared by: Simon Hartley, Senior Loss Adjuster\n\nTOTAL CLAIMED LOSS: £8,200,000\n\n(a) Incident response and forensics: £1,100,000. Covered; invoiced and verified.\n(b) Business interruption: £900,000. CONTESTED IN PART. The whole 100 MW site was offline for six weeks (21 March to 2 May): lost frequency response and trading revenue, less costs saved, plus NESO non-delivery charges. Meridian's adjusters argue that two to three weeks of a planned recertification were owed anyway because of the deferred SIS patch. My view: the full six weeks follow from the incident; a tampered safety system is revalidated whether or not it was ever patched.\n(c) Physical damage: £5,000,000. Replacement battery modules for Racks A1-A4 (Hall 1, 100 MWh). Halden Cell Systems withdrew its warranty on every module in those racks after the overcharge and temperatures of up to about 58°C. CONTESTED IN PART: Meridian's engineers say some modules could be requalified rather than replaced.\n(d) Revalidation and recommissioning: £800,000. SIS revalidation (including firmware 2.4.1), Hall 1 recommissioning and grid compliance retests. Meridian may treat about £180,000 (the patch work Albion had deferred) as betterment.\n(e) Third-party liability, Trent Water Services: £400,000, PROVISIONAL. A file the attacker wrote to the shared file server was opened on a Trent Water workstation. Trent Water has claimed from Albion its costs of cleaning that workstation and checking its pumping station control system. Liability and quantum depend on Trent Water's forensic findings.\n\nWARRANTIES AND THE INSURANCE ACT 2015 (for counsel; quantum is my remit, coverage is not):\n- The Act does not reduce cover in proportion to a warranty breach. Proportionate remedies apply to a failure of fair presentation at placement (section 8, Schedule 1), and nobody suggests Albion misrepresented the risk.\n- Section 10: cover is suspended while a warranty breach is unremedied. W-07 was unremedied from 31 December 2025, so Meridian can argue it has no liability for a loss on 21 March.\n- Section 11: Meridian cannot rely on a breach of a term aimed at a particular kind of loss if Albion shows the breach could not have increased the risk of the loss as it happened. That is hard for W-07, since the attacker came through the segmentation gaps, and arguable for W-03.\n- Albion will also argue waiver and estoppel: Meridian renewed in November 2025 knowing the deadline would be missed.\n\nMY RECOMMENDATION:\nPosition A1 (full coverage) is commercially generous but exposed.\nPosition A2, a negotiated partial settlement of about £6.1M, prices the litigation risk on both sides of the section 10, section 11 and waiver arguments, plus the contested modules and betterment. It is the most defensible commercial outcome.\nPosition A3 (decline on W-07) is legally arguable under section 10, but the renewal memo makes it the riskiest: likely arbitration and regulatory criticism.",
          "observations": "A sealed envelope. 'FAIRBRIDGE ASSOCIATES — INDEPENDENT LOSS ADJUSTMENT — PRIVILEGED AND CONFIDENTIAL'",
