          "text": "MERIDIAN FORENSIC CASE — EXHIBIT C — Warranty Compliance Evidence\n\nW-07 IT/OT SEGMENTATION: BREACHED\nQuarterly security posture reports (Q1-Q4 pre-incident) confirm all three IT/OT boundary deficiencies remained unremediated. Remediation deadline of 31 December 2025 passed under three months before the incident. Albion said at the November 2025 renewal meeting that the deadline would be missed. Extension request (ref WW-006) received 14 January 2026, after the deadline: under review, not approved, at time of incident.\n\nW-03 PATCH MANAGEMENT: ARGUABLE\nSIS firmware patch deferred for legitimate IEC 61511 reason — 8-week recertification, £180,000 cost. W-03 safety-constraint exception clause applies to the deferral decision.\nHOWEVER: the compensating control named in Albion's September 2024 risk assessment, monitoring by the managed SOC for lateral movement, never covered the SIS: the CastleTech contract excluded OT. Nothing isolated the SIS engineering port from the SCADA network either. The control was accepted in the risk register but never existed.\nDistinction: the deferral was legitimate; the failure to implement compensating controls was not. Deferral approved by J. Whitworth in September 2024 with a review date of March 2025; never reviewed.\nCAUSATION: the 03:22 SIS threshold change used the unauthenticated engineering protocol that the patch closes. Albion argues the patch could not reasonably have gone onto the certified SIL 2 controller in time, and that the attacker reached the SIS only through the W-07 segmentation gaps.\n\nW-09 ACCESS CONTROL: BREACHED\nContractor c.ellison left 14 months before the incident. Domain account locked; jump server local account missed by the leaver process, default password unchanged. Direct contributor to attack pathway.\n\nW-12 MSP SECURITY: LIKELY BREACHED\nCastleTech service account had cross-site privileges across Albion and Trent Water endpoints. Dormant accounts not revoked.",
