              "text": "MERIDIAN CYBER INSURANCE — UNDERWRITING FILE — MC-2023-ALBE-007\nCONFIDENTIAL — Internal Use Only\n\nPRE-INCEPTION ASSESSMENT (Dec 2024):\nAlbion Energy Storage presented with known IT/OT boundary deficiencies at application stage. The dual-homed historian and bidirectional jump server were identified by our technical underwriters as material risks.\n\nUNDERWRITING DECISION:\nCoverage offered on condition that IT/OT segmentation remediation is completed by 31 December 2025 (W-07 warranty condition). The underwriting team assessed this as a 'manageable near-term risk' given Albion's stated remediation commitment.\n\nRENEWAL MEMO (November 2025): CRITICAL\nTechnical underwriters reviewed Albion's Q2/Q3 security posture reports for the renewal due 15 January 2026. Confirmed: IT/OT segmentation remediation NOT completed; dual-homed historian still in service. At the renewal meeting Albion said the 31 December deadline would be missed and that an extension request would follow.\n\nDecision at renewal: RETAIN the account. W-07 warranty condition and deadline maintained. Premium uplift of 12% applied.\n\nRationale noted by underwriting committee: 'Albion's remediation programme is credible and in progress. An extension request is expected. Technical risk remains elevated but the account is commercially strategic.'\n\nSENIOR UNDERWRITER NOTE:\nThis file documents that Meridian had actual knowledge of the unremediated W-07 deficiency at renewal, and knew the deadline would be missed. We chose to retain the risk. The extension request (ref WW-006, received 14 January 2026) was not formally approved before the incident.\n\nIMPLICATION FOR CLAIM HANDLING:\nAny coverage position must account for Meridian's own knowledge and decision to renew. Declining on W-07 grounds after knowingly renewing the risk invites waiver and estoppel arguments, and Albion will also test whether each breach could have increased the risk of the loss that occurred (Insurance Act 2015, section 11).",
